<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.MirrorRead</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>harness-mirror-read</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THIS BUILD READS HOLDING REGISTERS AND NOTHING ELSE.

    A SEPARATE executable, for the same reason rig-read and rig-write are separate: neither can quietly
    acquire the other's half when the halves are not in the same assembly. Here the split is one level
    up — Harness.Wire owns both the read and the write, because the harness proper must write vectors
    and raise start bools, so this project narrows rather than excludes. IRegisterSource carries only a
    read verb and ModbusRegisterSource never hands the transport out, which means no call site above the
    adapter is holding anything that can write.

    *** THE CLAIM IS CHECKED, NOT DECLARED. *** MirrorReadStructureTests walks every method body in the
    compiled assembly and asserts that none of them names a write member of Harness.Wire, Harness.Map or
    NModbus, with a denominator (bodies examined > 0) and a live positive control (a planted class in the
    test assembly that DOES call WriteHoldingRegisters, which the same walk must find). A const bool with
    an Assert.False on it is what this replaces: measured 2026-08-14 on Harness.RigWrite, adding a class
    that constructed a live socket client left all 202 tests green.

    NModbus is referenced DIRECTLY, for one type: SlaveException. The boundary probe has to tell a
    Modbus exception response (the server received the request and refused it — the measurement) from a
    timeout (the server said nothing — no measurement at all). Collapsing those two would let a dropped
    packet be reported as proof of a boundary.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NModbus" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Wire\Harness.Wire.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\device-guard\DeviceGuard\DeviceGuard.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
